 will be known as Toto—the Prime Minister's own Toto. That is how weak this opposition really is. The fix is in. The hours are done— Sue Lines Senator Hanson-Young, resume your seat. I remind you that those in the other place need to be referred to by their correct titles. Senator Scarr? Paul Scarr Personal reflection—'roll over like a lapdog', and calling a member of the other place 'Toto', a dog. Sue Lines Senator Scarr, I will ask Senator Hanson-Young to withdraw that comment, and I remind you that I would prefer that offensive comments not be repeated by those calling points of order. Senator Hanson-Young, could you withdraw that comment, please. Sarah Hanson-Young Which bit? Sue Lines Senator Hanson-Young, could you withdraw that comment. Sarah Hanson-Young I'm happy to withdraw, President, but I'd like you to take away and reflect whether the word 'lapdog' is indeed now not— Sue Lines Senator Hanson-Young, I don't need to reflect. You have— Sarah Hanson-Young I'll write to you. I withdraw. Sue Lines Thank you. Please continue. Sarah Hanson-Young It is so clear that the opposition has rolled over, and the Prime Minister is ramming through legislation that satisfies the gambling industry. The people on the backbench of the Labor Party must be appalled. They m","result":"affirmative","slug":"division-federal-senate-10676","source_url":"https://theyvoteforyou.org.au/divisions/senate/2026-08-18/1"}
